  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":68,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A market cooperative weighs 50 sacks of rice. No single sack is known, but the clerk''s tally sheet survives.\\n\\na Calculate the estimated mean mass of the 50 sacks, and explain why it is an estimate. (2)\\n\\nb The cooperative sells rice at 600 FCFA per kilogram. Estimate the total value of the 50 sacks. (1)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A market cooperative weighs 50 sacks of rice. No single sack is known, but the clerk''s tally sheet survives."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Calculate the estimated mean mass of the 50 sacks, and explain why it is an estimate. (2)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b The cooperative sells rice at 600 FCFA per kilogram. Estimate the total value of the 50 sacks. (1)"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mass m (kg)"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequency"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40 ≤ m < 45"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"45 ≤ m < 50"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"50 ≤ m < 55"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"18"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"55 ≤ m < 60"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"60 ≤ m < 65"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Midpoints: 42.5, 47.5, 52.5, 57.5, 62.5 kg. Σfx = 42.5×5 + 47.5×12 + 52.5×18 + 57.5×10 + 62.5×5 = 212.5 + 570 + 945 + 575 + 312.5 = 2615 kg. Estimated mean = 2615 ÷ 50 = 52.3 kg. It is an estimate because the individual masses are not recorded — using class midpoints assumes every sack in a class weighs exactly the class midpoint, which is not true in general. (b) Estimated total mass = 50 × 52.3 = 2615 kg; total value = 2615 × 600 = 1 569 000 FCFA.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the estimated mean 52.3 kg (method: midpoints + Σfx ÷ 50). 1 mark for the explanation (midpoints replace unknown values; grouped data). 1 mark for ≈1 569 000 FCFA (accept ±1 000; accept 1.57 × 10⁶). A candidate computing total value from their own mean × 50 × 600 gets full marks for consistent working.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2615 kg’ shown as a step; value in scientific notation; mean 52.3 kg.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Grouped Frequency Tables","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":68,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Midpoints, Σfx ÷ 50, then multiply by 600.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) estimated mean","prompt":"Calculate the estimated mean mass of the 50 sacks.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Midpoints: 42.5, 47.5, 52.5, 57.5, 62.5.","Multiply each midpoint by its frequency, add, then divide by 50."],"answer":52.3,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) why an estimate","prompt":"Explain why it is an estimate.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["No individual masses are known — the table only shows counts per class.","Using midpoints assumes every sack in a class has the class’s mid value."],"accepted":["the individual masses are unknown; the midpoints replace all values in a class, so the mean is an approximation","grouped data: midpoints stand in for every sack in the class, which may not reflect the true values"],"keywords":[["midpoint"],["unknown","not known","no individual","individual","actual","real"],["approximate","estimate","approximat","guess","assume"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b","label":"(b) total value","prompt":"Estimate the total value of the 50 sacks at 600 FCFA per kilogram.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Total mass ≈ 2615 kg (= 50 × 52.3).","Multiply your total mass by 600 FCFA per kg."],"answer":1569000,"tolerance":1000,"unit":"FCFA"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Midpoints: 42.5, 47.5, 52.5, 57.5, 62.5 kg. Σfx = 42.5×5 + 47.5×12 + 52.5×18 + 57.5×10 + 62.5×5 = 212.5 + 570 + 945 + 575 + 312.5 = 2615 kg. Estimated mean = 2615 ÷ 50 = 52.3 kg. It is an estimate because the individual masses are not recorded — using class midpoints assumes every sack in a class weighs exactly the class midpoint, which is not true in general. (b) Estimated total mass = 50 × 52.3 = 2615 kg; total value = 2615 × 600 = 1 569 000 FCFA.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the estimated mean 52.3 kg (method: midpoints + Σfx ÷ 50). 1 mark for the explanation (midpoints replace unknown values; grouped data). 1 mark for ≈1 569 000 FCFA (accept ±1 000; accept 1.57 × 10⁶). A candidate computing total value from their own mean × 50 × 600 gets full marks for consistent working.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2615 kg’ shown as a step; value in scientific notation; mean 52.3 kg.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Midpoints: 42.5, 47.5, 52.5, 57.5, 62.5 kg. Σfx = 42.5×5 + 47.5×12 + 52.5×18 + 57.5×10 + 62.5×5 = 212.5 + 570 + 945 + 575 + 312.5 = 2615 kg. Estimated mean = 2615 ÷ 50 = 52.3 kg. It is an estimate because the individual masses are not recorded — using class midpoints assumes every sack in a class weighs exactly the class midpoint, which is not true in general. (b) Estimated total mass = 50 × 52.3 = 2615 kg; total value = 2615 × 600 = 1 569 000 FCFA.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the estimated mean 52.3 kg (method: midpoints + Σfx ÷ 50). 1 mark for the explanation (midpoints replace unknown values; grouped data). 1 mark for ≈1 569 000 FCFA (accept ±1 000; accept 1.57 × 10⁶). A candidate computing total value from their own mean × 50 × 600 gets full marks for consistent working.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2615 kg’ shown as a step; value in scientific notation; mean 52.3 kg.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Midpoints: 42.5, 47.5, 52.5, 57.5, 62.5 kg. Σfx = 42.5×5 + 47.5×12 + 52.5×18 + 57.5×10 + 62.5×5 = 212.5 + 570 + 945 + 575 + 312.5 = 2615 kg. Estimated mean = 2615 ÷ 50 = 52.3 kg. It is an estimate because the individual masses are not recorded — using class midpoints assumes every sack in a class weighs exactly the class midpoint, which is not true in general. (b) Estimated total mass = 50 × 52.3 = 2615 kg; total value = 2615 × 600 = 1 569 000 FCFA.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the estimated mean 52.3 kg (method: midpoints + Σfx ÷ 50). 1 mark for the explanation (midpoints replace unknown values; grouped data). 1 mark for ≈1 569 000 FCFA (accept ±1 000; accept 1.57 × 10⁶). A candidate computing total value from their own mean × 50 × 600 gets full marks for consistent working.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2615 kg’ shown as a step; value in scientific notation; mean 52.3 kg."},{"n":69,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A battery factory in Cotonou finds that the lifetime of its batteries is normally distributed with mean 480 hours and standard deviation 40 hours. Each battery is guaranteed for 400 hours.\\n\\na Find the percentage of batteries that fail before the guarantee runs out, then estimate how many of the 8 000 batteries made in a day will be replaced. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A battery factory in Cotonou finds that the lifetime of its batteries is normally distributed with mean 480 hours and standard deviation 40 hours. Each battery is guaranteed for 400 hours."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Find the percentage of batteries that fail before the guarantee runs out, then estimate how many of the 8 000 batteries made in a day will be replaced. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — 400 hours = 480 − 2 × 40 = μ − 2σ. From the 68–95–99.7 rule, 95% of batteries lie within 2σ, so 2.5% lie below μ − 2σ and fail before the guarantee runs out (the exact normal probability is 2.275%). Expected replacements per day: 2.5% × 8000 = 200 batteries (using the exact figure: 0.0228 × 8000 ≈ 182).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈2.5% (accept 2.3% or 2.275%, or any answer derived consistently from z = −2 and a recognised normal-tail method). 1 mark for ≈200 (or ≈182 with the exact percentage; accept any number consistent with the candidate’s own percentage, within 5%). Full method credit for showing 400 = μ − 2σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘200 batteries’, ‘182 (exact)’; percentage answers with stated method.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":69,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"400 is μ − 2σ — find the left tail, then take 2.5% of 8000.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) percentage failing before guarantee","prompt":"Find the percentage of batteries that fail before 400 hours.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["400 = 480 − 2 × 40 = μ − 2σ.","Below μ − 2σ lies an outer tail — the 95% band spans μ ± 2σ, so split what remains on one side."],"accepted":["2.5% — by the 68-95-99.7 rule: 400 h = μ − 2σ, so the left tail is (100 − 95) ÷ 2 = 2.5%","2.5 % (or 2.275% with the exact normal table)","about 2.5 percent"],"keywords":[["2.5","2,5","2.28","2.3"],["95","68-95-99.7","68–95–99.7","two standard","2σ","empirical","z = -2","z=-2"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) expected number replaced","prompt":"Estimate how many of the 8 000 batteries made in a day will be replaced.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Multiply your own percentage from part (a) by the total produced.","Use the fraction of bad batteries you found in (a), scaled to 8 000 units."],"answer":200,"tolerance":20,"unit":"batteries"}],"marking":"Model answer — 400 hours = 480 − 2 × 40 = μ − 2σ. From the 68–95–99.7 rule, 95% of batteries lie within 2σ, so 2.5% lie below μ − 2σ and fail before the guarantee runs out (the exact normal probability is 2.275%). Expected replacements per day: 2.5% × 8000 = 200 batteries (using the exact figure: 0.0228 × 8000 ≈ 182).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈2.5% (accept 2.3% or 2.275%, or any answer derived consistently from z = −2 and a recognised normal-tail method). 1 mark for ≈200 (or ≈182 with the exact percentage; accept any number consistent with the candidate’s own percentage, within 5%). Full method credit for showing 400 = μ − 2σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘200 batteries’, ‘182 (exact)’; percentage answers with stated method.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — 400 hours = 480 − 2 × 40 = μ − 2σ. From the 68–95–99.7 rule, 95% of batteries lie within 2σ, so 2.5% lie below μ − 2σ and fail before the guarantee runs out (the exact normal probability is 2.275%). Expected replacements per day: 2.5% × 8000 = 200 batteries (using the exact figure: 0.0228 × 8000 ≈ 182).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈2.5% (accept 2.3% or 2.275%, or any answer derived consistently from z = −2 and a recognised normal-tail method). 1 mark for ≈200 (or ≈182 with the exact percentage; accept any number consistent with the candidate’s own percentage, within 5%). Full method credit for showing 400 = μ − 2σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘200 batteries’, ‘182 (exact)’; percentage answers with stated method.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — 400 hours = 480 − 2 × 40 = μ − 2σ. From the 68–95–99.7 rule, 95% of batteries lie within 2σ, so 2.5% lie below μ − 2σ and fail before the guarantee runs out (the exact normal probability is 2.275%). Expected replacements per day: 2.5% × 8000 = 200 batteries (using the exact figure: 0.0228 × 8000 ≈ 182).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈2.5% (accept 2.3% or 2.275%, or any answer derived consistently from z = −2 and a recognised normal-tail method). 1 mark for ≈200 (or ≈182 with the exact percentage; accept any number consistent with the candidate’s own percentage, within 5%). Full method credit for showing 400 = μ − 2σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘200 batteries’, ‘182 (exact)’; percentage answers with stated method."},{"n":70,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A teacher claims that pupils who sit at the front of the class score higher than pupils who sit at the back. The front row''s marks have a mean of 74 and a standard deviation of 9; the back row''s have a mean of 66 and a standard deviation of 16. Both rows contain 10 pupils.\\n\\na Compare the means and say whether the teacher''s claim is supported by them. (1)\\n\\nb State one other explanation for the difference in the means, and say what the teacher would need to do to test it. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A teacher claims that pupils who sit at the front of the class score higher than pupils who sit at the back. The front row''s marks have a mean of 74 and a standard deviation of 9; the back row''s have a mean of 66 and a standard deviation of 16. Both rows contain 10 pupils."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Compare the means and say whether the teacher''s claim is supported by them. (1)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b State one other explanation for the difference in the means, and say what the teacher would need to do to test it. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Front row mean 74 vs back row mean 66: a gap of 8 marks in the direction of the claim — the means support the claim descriptively for these two rows. But caution is needed: only 10 pupils per row and the back row’s σ = 16 (the rows’ scores overlap heavily) mean an 8-mark gap can easily arise by chance; the means alone do not prove seating causes the gap. (b) Other explanation (any one): self-selection — abler or more motivated pupils choose the front seats; or the front row happened to get different/better-delivered material; or baseline ability differed. To test it: assign seating at random for several assessments and compare the resulting class means (randomised experiment), or collect baseline (pre-test) data and compare the rows’ progress controlling for prior attainment; a larger sample across classes would strengthen the conclusion.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 1 mark: the 8-mark comparison in the claimed direction (accept ‘supported descriptively’ with any caveat, or ‘supported on these means alone’). (b) 2 marks: 1 for a plausible alternative explanation (self-selection, prior ability, motivation, teaching/material differences) and 1 for an appropriate test (randomised seating, baseline control or larger multi-class sample).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — any coherent confound + matching test; ‘compare gains, not raw scores’; ‘switch seats halfway through term and retest’.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":70,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Means say what these rows scored — what would PROVE seating caused it?","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) compare the means","prompt":"Compare the means and say whether the teacher''s claim is supported by them.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["74 vs 66 — the gap is 8 marks in the direction of the claim.","But are 10 pupils per row enough, and what does the back row’s σ = 16 imply?"],"accepted":["The front row’s mean (74) is 8 marks higher than the back row’s (66), so on average the claim is directionally supported — but 10 pupils per row and a large σ of 16 in the back row mean the difference is easily due to chance, so the means alone are weak evidence","74 against 66, a gap of 8 marks — supports the claim descriptively, though with only 10 pupils per row and wide overlap (σ 16) it is not convincing proof"],"keywords":[["74"],["66"],["8","eight","gap","higher","difference"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) other explanation","prompt":"State one other explanation for the difference in the means.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Who chooses the front seats?","Motivated/abler pupils may sit at the front — seating does not cause the scores."],"accepted":["self-selection — more able or motivated pupils choose the front seats, so the rows differ in ability rather than in seating","confounding by prior ability/motivation: front-row pupils were already stronger; or the front row had different teaching/materials"],"keywords":[["self-select","chose","choose","choice","motivat","abler","stronger","able","prior","ability","confoun","different teach","different materia","health","vision","hear"]],"minKeywordGroups":1},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) how to test it","prompt":"Say what the teacher would need to do to test it.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Change the seating at random and re-measure.","Or collect baseline scores before seating and compare gain, controlling for ability."],"accepted":["randomise seating (assign pupils to front/back at random for several tests) and compare the resulting means; or match the rows on prior attainment and compare","run a randomised experiment — swap/assign seats at random and see if the gap follows the seating; or control for prior attainment with baseline data and a larger sample"],"keywords":[["random","swap","assign","experiment","control","baseline","prior","larger sample","more data","over time","multiple tests"]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Front row mean 74 vs back row mean 66: a gap of 8 marks in the direction of the claim — the means support the claim descriptively for these two rows. But caution is needed: only 10 pupils per row and the back row’s σ = 16 (the rows’ scores overlap heavily) mean an 8-mark gap can easily arise by chance; the means alone do not prove seating causes the gap. (b) Other explanation (any one): self-selection — abler or more motivated pupils choose the front seats; or the front row happened to get different/better-delivered material; or baseline ability differed. To test it: assign seating at random for several assessments and compare the resulting class means (randomised experiment), or collect baseline (pre-test) data and compare the rows’ progress controlling for prior attainment; a larger sample across classes would strengthen the conclusion.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 1 mark: the 8-mark comparison in the claimed direction (accept ‘supported descriptively’ with any caveat, or ‘supported on these means alone’). (b) 2 marks: 1 for a plausible alternative explanation (self-selection, prior ability, motivation, teaching/material differences) and 1 for an appropriate test (randomised seating, baseline control or larger multi-class sample).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — any coherent confound + matching test; ‘compare gains, not raw scores’; ‘switch seats halfway through term and retest’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Front row mean 74 vs back row mean 66: a gap of 8 marks in the direction of the claim — the means support the claim descriptively for these two rows. But caution is needed: only 10 pupils per row and the back row’s σ = 16 (the rows’ scores overlap heavily) mean an 8-mark gap can easily arise by chance; the means alone do not prove seating causes the gap. (b) Other explanation (any one): self-selection — abler or more motivated pupils choose the front seats; or the front row happened to get different/better-delivered material; or baseline ability differed. To test it: assign seating at random for several assessments and compare the resulting class means (randomised experiment), or collect baseline (pre-test) data and compare the rows’ progress controlling for prior attainment; a larger sample across classes would s